---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const description = "How Insidia handles scope, findings, and secrets. The CLI stays on your machine. Insidia Cloud encrypts hosted findings per organization.";
---
<Base title="Security | Insidia Labs" description={description} path="/security">
  <Header />
  <main id="content" class="wrap" style="padding:3rem 0 4rem">
    <h1>Security</h1>
    <p class="lede">This website does not run scans and does not store findings. A waitlist email is a marketing lead, kept out of the product database.</p>
    <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <article class="card">
        <h2>CLI scope</h2>
        <p>Insidia scans only hosts listed in <code>insidia.yaml</code>. Localhost is the default. Any other host needs <code>authorized: true</code>, which you set. Rate limits are on by default.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2>What leaves the machine</h2>
        <p>A local scan talks to your target and, if you configured one, your model endpoint. No telemetry. Secrets in evidence are masked, for example [AWS_ACCESS_KEY len=20 fp=3f9a1c07].</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2>Insidia Cloud</h2>
        <p>When the hosted service is open, findings are stored with per-organization envelope encryption. They are not used as training data. You can self-host that code on your own GPUs.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <section class="block">
      <h2>Testing only what you may test</h2>
      <p>Point Insidia at systems you own or have written permission to test. Design-partner Cloud tests run against staging, and only after that authorization. Report a vulnerability in Insidia itself to <a href="mailto:insidialabs@gmail.com?subject=Security%20question">insidialabs@gmail.com</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
